<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Custom of Paris</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Coutume de Paris</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Custom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> was the body of customary law of the region of Paris, first written down in 1510 and revised in 1580. Made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> in 1664, it governed property, inheritance, marriage and the seigneurial system in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, survived the British conquest under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec-act">Quebec Act</a> of 1774 and remained the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> law until the Civil Code of 1866.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/custom-of-paris.jpg" alt="Custom of Paris" width="320" height="512" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Guy Carleton (died 1808) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Custom_of_Paris_as_Practised_in_Quebec_during_the_French_government.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
